cff-version: 1.2.0
type: dataset
message: >-
  If you use HITL-IoT in your research, please cite the associated
  TBench research article listed under preferred-citation and cite
  the version-specific dataset DOI once the v1.1 Zenodo release is published.
title: "HITL-IoT: A Reproducible Dataset and Benchmark for Trustworthy Human-AI Oversight and Selective Deferral in IoT Cybersecurity"
authors:
  - family-names: Wakili
    given-names: Abubakar
    affiliation: Euromed University of Fez
  - family-names: Idris
    given-names: Muhammad
    affiliation: University of Passau
  - family-names: Bakkali
    given-names: Sara
    affiliation: Euromed University of Fez
version: "1.1"
license: CC-BY-4.0
abstract: >-
  HITL-IoT is a reproducible, deterministically generated Internet of
  Things cybersecurity dataset and benchmark for intrusion detection,
  probability calibration, selective prediction and oversight allocation.
  It contains 127,845 simulated network-flow records and 10,227 records
  of generated oversight-profile decisions, confidence, latency and
  rationales. The benchmark provides fixed evaluation splits and a
  leakage-safe feature policy. The oversight records are not observations
  from human analysts.
repository-code: "https://github.com/wakili-research/HITL-IoT-Dataset"
url: "https://github.com/wakili-research/HITL-IoT-Dataset"
keywords:
  - Probability calibration
  - Learning to defer
  - IoT cybersecurity
  - Intrusion detection
  - Trustworthy AI
  - Human-AI collaboration
  - Human oversight
  - Selective deferral
  - Reproducible benchmark
